    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form",
    "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+12x-12y=12?$",
    "worked_solution": "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+12x-12y & =12 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-12y-3{x}^{2}+12x & =12 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-6y]-3[{x}^{2}-4x] & =12 \\\\ 2[(y-3)^{2}-9]-3[(x-2)^{2}-4] & =12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-18-3(x-2)^{2}+12 & =12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-3(x-2)^{2} & =12+18-12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-3(x-2)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(2,3).$",
